      "sections": {
        "definition": "<p>Beta-aminoisobutyric acid (BAIBA) is a small molecule metabolite produced primarily from the catabolism of valine and thymine in skeletal muscle. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37313446\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 37313446</a>) It exists as two enantiomers (R-BAIBA and S-BAIBA), with R-BAIBA being approximately 67 times more abundant in human plasma than S-BAIBA. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31611815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 31611815</a>)</p>",
        "biologicalFunction": "<p>BAIBA functions as a signaling metabolite with multiple biological effects, including regulation of lipid and glucose metabolism, promotion of white adipose tissue browning, enhancement of hepatic fatty acid oxidation, anti-inflammatory properties, and protection against oxidative stress. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37313446\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 37313446</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24411942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 24411942</a>) It acts through several mechanisms, including activation of AMPK signaling and PPARα-mediated pathways. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24411942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 24411942</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31277947\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 31277947</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38219388\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 38219388</a>) BAIBA also demonstrates protective effects on bone by preventing osteocyte apoptosis through the Mas-Related G Protein-Coupled Receptor Type D (MRGPRD). (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29425508\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 29425508</a>)</p>",
        "exerkineClassification": "<p>BAIBA is considered an exerkine (also termed a myokine). (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37313446\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 37313446</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38219388\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 38219388</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29177585\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 29177585</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40157803\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 40157803</a>) This classification is supported by several lines of evidence:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>BAIBA is produced and secreted by skeletal muscle during exercise, with plasma levels increasing acutely following aerobic exercise (13% increase in R-BAIBA and 20% increase in S-BAIBA after one hour of exercise at 40% peak power) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31611815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 31611815</a>)</li>\n<li>It is released from myocytes with forced expression of PGC-1α, a key regulator of the exercise response in muscle (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24411942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 24411942</a>)</li>\n<li>BAIBA mediates inter-organ communication, acting on distant tissues including adipose tissue, liver, bone, and cardiovascular system to exert systemic metabolic effects (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24411942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 24411942</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29177585\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 29177585</a>) (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28441626\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 28441626</a>)</li>\n<li>In humans, plasma BAIBA concentrations are inversely associated with cardiometabolic risk factors (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24411942\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 24411942</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The molecule has been described as a potential \"exercise pill\" due to its ability to confer metabolic benefits similar to those of exercise. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37313446\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 37313446</a>)</p>",
        "therapeuticPotential": "<p>BAIBA has shown therapeutic effects in type 2 diabetes and metabolic disorders. In diabetic mice, oral BAIBA administration reduced blood glucose and lipid levels, attenuated hepatic insulin resistance and lipid accumulation, and reduced hepatic endoplasmic reticulum stress and apoptosis through AMPK-dependent mechanisms. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26907958\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 26907958</a>) These metabolic benefits extend to adipose tissue, where BAIBA attenuated LPS-induced inflammation and insulin resistance in adipocytes, also via AMPK-mediated pathways. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29592806\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 29592806</a>)</p>\n\n<p>The compound has been characterized as a signaling metabolite and potential \"exercise pill\" due to its production during exercise and involvement in regulating lipid, glucose, and bone metabolism, as well as inflammation and oxidative stress. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37313446\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 37313446</a>) BAIBA enhances fatty acid oxidation and reduces body weight through increased leptin production by white adipose tissue. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19735301\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 19735301</a>)</p>\n\n<p>Safety data support its therapeutic development. A 90-day subchronic toxicity study in rats established a no-observed-adverse-effect level (NOAEL) of 900 mg/kg/day, with no treatment-related adverse effects observed. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35549583\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 35549583</a>)</p>\n\n<p>Additional therapeutic applications have been explored in neuropsychiatric conditions. BAIBA functions as a gliotransmitter that activates GABA-B and group-III metabotropic glutamate receptors, potentially contributing to clozapine's efficacy in treatment-resistant schizophrenia. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37759688\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 37759688</a>)</p>\n\n<p>BAIBA has also been investigated as a biomarker in malignancy, with elevated urinary concentrations found in patients with leukemia and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, though this represents diagnostic rather than therapeutic utility. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3652458\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 3652458</a>)</p>\n\n<p>Human studies and reviews have identified BAIBA-related metabolites as part of metabolic pathways associated with type 2 diabetes development, further supporting its relevance in metabolic disease therapeutics. (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34325838\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMID: 34325838</a>)</p>"
      },
